WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:02.133
CAITLIN CASSIDY: My
name is Caitlin Cassidy,

00:00:02.133 --> 00:00:04.380
and my SuperUROP
project is about helping

00:00:04.380 --> 00:00:07.900
teachers give better
feedback on students' code.

00:00:07.900 --> 00:00:11.340
So let's take a class at
MIT that's teaching students

00:00:11.340 --> 00:00:13.071
how to program in Java.

00:00:13.071 --> 00:00:15.570
Teachers already do a great job
of giving students feedback.

00:00:15.570 --> 00:00:17.400
So they look at
every student's code

00:00:17.400 --> 00:00:19.350
and give them
personalized feedback.

00:00:19.350 --> 00:00:21.500
And they also give students
feedback immediately,

00:00:21.500 --> 00:00:23.430
so that day or the day after.

00:00:23.430 --> 00:00:26.490
So my project is about helping
teachers give better feedback

00:00:26.490 --> 00:00:28.350
by changing what they look at.

00:00:28.350 --> 00:00:30.220
Typically, when
teachers grade code

00:00:30.220 --> 00:00:32.460
they only look at a
student's final code.

00:00:32.460 --> 00:00:34.500
And we're investigating
whether if teachers

00:00:34.500 --> 00:00:36.330
can see, in addition
to final code,

00:00:36.330 --> 00:00:38.860
all the steps that students
took along the way,

00:00:38.860 --> 00:00:41.550
whether teachers can give
students better feedback.

00:00:41.550 --> 00:00:44.280
So our final interface is
this one in the middle.

00:00:44.280 --> 00:00:47.820
So on this left side, we can see
all the students' final code.

00:00:47.820 --> 00:00:49.830
So it's as if they
pressed Submit at the end.

00:00:49.830 --> 00:00:51.894
The code on the left
is their final code.

00:00:51.894 --> 00:00:53.310
But in addition,
we have this code

00:00:53.310 --> 00:00:55.860
on the right side over here
that you can see in red,

00:00:55.860 --> 00:00:58.500
and that's code that the
students typed and then deleted

00:00:58.500 --> 00:00:59.850
at some point in the middle.

00:00:59.850 --> 00:01:02.430
So it highlights all the
steps that students took along

00:01:02.430 --> 00:01:04.750
the way to get to
the final product.

00:01:04.750 --> 00:01:06.750
To test whether this
actually improves feedback,

00:01:06.750 --> 00:01:10.380
we ran an experiment
in an MIT classroom.

00:01:10.380 --> 00:01:12.540
And we found two metrics
that say that teachers

00:01:12.540 --> 00:01:13.950
give longer feedback.

00:01:13.950 --> 00:01:16.140
So on average, if
they were looking

00:01:16.140 --> 00:01:19.410
at an old interface that
just showed final code,

00:01:19.410 --> 00:01:23.100
teachers use about 43 characters
on average to give feedback.

00:01:23.100 --> 00:01:24.750
And now, with our
interface, they

00:01:24.750 --> 00:01:27.180
give 49 and 1/2 characters.

00:01:27.180 --> 00:01:30.750
In addition, before,
about 80% of comments

00:01:30.750 --> 00:01:33.660
were above 10 characters,
which is already great.

00:01:33.660 --> 00:01:36.720
So less than 10 is maybe if
they said "Good" or "Great job"

00:01:36.720 --> 00:01:37.830
as a comment only.

00:01:37.830 --> 00:01:39.975
So 80% above that is awesome.

00:01:39.975 --> 00:01:41.790
But we increased that to 90%.

00:01:41.790 --> 00:01:43.500
So we're seeing
much longer feedback

00:01:43.500 --> 00:01:47.520
given by TAs or teachers who
can see a student's code history

00:01:47.520 --> 00:01:49.440
as well as their final code.

00:01:49.440 --> 00:01:50.970
We also did a
qualitative assessment

00:01:50.970 --> 00:01:52.240
about teachers' comments.

00:01:52.240 --> 00:01:59.340
So some students were graded
once without the extra code.

00:01:59.340 --> 00:02:02.370
So 30% of students
were graded once

00:02:02.370 --> 00:02:05.940
without the extra
visualization and once with.

00:02:05.940 --> 00:02:08.580
And qualitatively, looking
at all those feedback,

00:02:08.580 --> 00:02:11.430
we saw that teachers did
give better comments.

00:02:11.430 --> 00:02:14.665
So as one example over
here, if the teachers only

00:02:14.665 --> 00:02:17.040
looked at final code, they
just thought that the students

00:02:17.040 --> 00:02:18.556
didn't do anything.

00:02:18.556 --> 00:02:20.430
But when we can see that
the student actually

00:02:20.430 --> 00:02:22.369
did something, shown
in red on the right,

00:02:22.369 --> 00:02:23.910
the teacher said,
"Good start, you're

00:02:23.910 --> 00:02:25.993
on the right track," which
is much better feedback

00:02:25.993 --> 00:02:28.940
for the student and is going
to improve their learning.

